r/50">  <p>The first time I set up wireless monitor mode capture, I made ev=
ery mistake in sequence =E2=80=94 wrong channel, no radiotap headers, captu=
ring in managed mode, then wondering why I could not see any probe requests=
. This article is the workflow I developed after enough failed captures: th=
e platform-specific monitor mode setup, the display filters that actually m=
atter, and the frame sequences to look for when diagnosing why a connection=
 is failing.</p>
<p>If you are searching for how to do Wi-Fi packet capture with Wireshark, =
this is the full path: 802.11 capture (sometimes called Wi-Fi sniffing) on =
macOS, Linux and Windows, the capture filters to apply while recording, and=
 the display filters to apply afterwards.</p>
<h2 id=3D"why-wireless-capture-is-different">Why Wireless Capture Is Differ=
ent<a href=3D"https://sageaxe.com/blog/wireshark-wifi-packet-capture#why-wi=
reless-capture-is-different" class=3D"heading-anchor" aria-label=3D"Copy li=
nk to this section"><svg width=3D"15" height=3D"15" viewBox=3D"0 0 24 24" f=
ill=3D"none" stroke=3D"currentColor" stroke-width=3D"2" stroke-linecap=3D"r=
ound" stroke-linejoin=3D"round" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=3D"M10 13a5 5 =
0 007.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 00-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"></path><path d=3D"M14 11a5 5=
 0 00-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 007.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"></path></svg></a></h2>
<p>Wired packet capture is straightforward =E2=80=94 plug into a mirror por=
t or tap, and you see every frame on the wire. Wireless capture is fundamen=
tally different because of the shared medium. A standard Wi-Fi adapter in m=
anaged mode only delivers frames destined for its own MAC address or broadc=
ast/multicast frames on the BSS it has joined. Management frames from other=
 BSSes, control frames like ACKs and RTS/CTS, and data frames between other=
 clients are all invisible [IEEE 802.11-2020, Section 5.2].</p>
<p>To see everything happening on a channel, the adapter must be placed in =
<strong>monitor mode</strong>. In monitor mode, the adapter does not associ=
ate with any access point. Instead, it captures every frame it receives on =
its current channel, regardless of BSSID or destination address. This inclu=
des beacons, probe requests, probe responses, association frames, authentic=
ation frames, data frames, and control frames [Wireshark Wiki, =E2=80=9CWLA=
N (IEEE 802.11) Capture Setup=E2=80=9D].</p>
<p>This article covers the complete workflow: configuring monitor mode on e=
ach major operating system, setting effective capture and display filters, =
and systematically analyzing the frames that matter for troubleshooting.</p=
>
<h2 id=3D"monitor-mode-setup">Monitor Mode Setup<a href=3D"https://sageaxe.=
