4-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 007.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"></path></svg></a></h3>
<p>macOS has built-in monitor mode support through the Wireless Diagnostics=
 framework. The fastest method uses the <code>airport</code> command-line u=
tility.</p>
<p><strong>Method 1: airport command</strong></p>
<pre class=3D"astro-code github-dark-dimmed" style=3D"background-color:#222=
72e;color:#adbac7; overflow-x: auto;" tabindex=3D"0" data-language=3D"bash"=
><code><span class=3D"line"><span style=3D"color:#768390"># Identify the wi=
reless interface (usually en0)</span></span>
<span class=3D"line"><span style=3D"color:#F69D50">networksetup</span><span=
 style=3D"color:#6CB6FF"> -listallhardwareports</span></span>
<span class=3D"line"></span>
<span class=3D"line"><span style=3D"color:#768390"># Disassociate from any =
current network</span></span>
<span class=3D"line"><span style=3D"color:#F69D50">sudo</span><span style=
=3D"color:#96D0FF"> /System/Library/PrivateFrameworks/Apple80211.framework/=
Versions/Current/Resources/airport</span><span style=3D"color:#6CB6FF"> -z<=
/span></span>
<span class=3D"line"></span>
<span class=3D"line"><span style=3D"color:#768390"># Set the channel (e.g.,=
 channel 36 with 80 MHz width)</span></span>
<span class=3D"line"><span style=3D"color:#F69D50">sudo</span><span style=
=3D"color:#96D0FF"> /System/Library/PrivateFrameworks/Apple80211.framework/=
Versions/Current/Resources/airport</span><span style=3D"color:#6CB6FF"> --c=
hannel=3D36</span></span>
<span class=3D"line"></span>
<span class=3D"line"><span style=3D"color:#768390"># Enable monitor mode vi=
a Wireshark</span></span>
<span class=3D"line"><span style=3D"color:#768390"># In Wireshark: Capture =
&gt; Options &gt; select en0 &gt; check "Monitor mode"</span></span></code>=
<button class=3D"copy-code-btn">Copy</button></pre>
<p><strong>If <code>airport</code> returns =E2=80=9Ccommand not found=E2=80=
=9D</strong>: Apple has deprecated this utility and it is missing on curren=
t macOS releases, so Method 1 will not work there. Use Method 2 below, whic=
h does not depend on it, or capture in monitor mode directly with <code>sud=
o tcpdump -I -i en0 -w capture.pcap</code> (the <code>-I</code> flag puts t=
he interface in monitor mode). Note that with <code>tcpdump -I</code> the a=
dapter stays on whatever channel it was already using; Wireless Diagnostics=
 is the route that lets you pick the channel and width.</p>
<p><strong>Method 2: Wireless Diagnostics GUI</strong></p>
<p>Open Wireless Diagnostics (Option-click the Wi-Fi icon, select =E2=80=9C=
Open Wireless Diagnostics=E2=80=9D), then use Window &gt; Sniffer. Select t=
he channel and channel width, click Start, and the tool captures to a <code=
>.pcap</code> file. This file can then be opened in Wireshark [Apple Develo=
per Documentation, =E2=80=9CWireless Diagnostics=E2=80=9D].</p>
<p><strong>macOS Limitations</strong>: The built-in Wi-Fi adapter captures =
on one channel at a time. There is no channel hopping. The adapter must be =
disconnected from any network during capture. On Apple Silicon Macs, the ad=
apter supports capture on 2.4 GHz, 5 GHz, and 6 GHz bands with up to 160 MH=
z channel width.</p>
