<h3 id=3D"what-to-look-for">What to Look For<a href=3D"https://sageaxe.com/=
blog/wireshark-wifi-packet-capture#what-to-look-for" class=3D"heading-ancho=
r" aria-label=3D"Copy link to this section"><svg width=3D"15" height=3D"15"=
 viewBox=3D"0 0 24 24" fill=3D"none" stroke=3D"currentColor" stroke-width=
=3D"2" stroke-linecap=3D"round" stroke-linejoin=3D"round" aria-hidden=3D"tr=
ue"><path d=3D"M10 13a5 5 0 007.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 00-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"></=
path><path d=3D"M14 11a5 5 0 00-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 007.07 7.07l1.71-1.71">=
</path></svg></a></h3>
<p><strong>Missing Probe Responses</strong>: If the client sends probe requ=
ests but receives no response, the AP may be filtering probes (band steerin=
g), the client may be on the wrong channel, or the AP may not be broadcasti=
ng the requested SSID. Filter with:</p>
<pre class=3D"astro-code github-dark-dimmed" style=3D"background-color:#222=
72e;color:#adbac7; overflow-x: auto;" tabindex=3D"0" data-language=3D"plain=
text"><code><span class=3D"line"><span>(wlan.fc.type_subtype =3D=3D 0x04 ||=
 wlan.fc.type_subtype =3D=3D 0x05) &amp;&amp; wlan.sa =3D=3D &lt;client_mac=
&gt;</span></span></code><button class=3D"copy-code-btn">Copy</button></pre=
>
<p><strong>Authentication Rejection</strong>: If the AP responds to the aut=
hentication request with a non-zero status code, the client is being reject=
ed before association. Status code 13 indicates the authentication algorith=
m requested is not supported by the AP (e.g. client sending Open System aut=
h to a SAE-only BSS). Status code 17 means the AP is at maximum capacity an=
d cannot handle additional STAs [IEEE 802.11-2020, Table 9-50].</p>
<p><strong>Association Rejection</strong>: The association response include=
s a status code. Status code 12 means association denied due to reason outs=
ide the scope of the standard. Status code 17 means the AP is at capacity. =
Check the RSN Information Element in the association request =E2=80=94 if t=
he client proposes a cipher suite the AP does not support, association will=
 fail.</p>
<p><strong>4-Way Handshake Failure</strong>: If the association succeeds bu=
t the 4-way handshake fails, the problem is usually a PSK mismatch (for WPA=
2-Personal) or a certificate/credential issue (for WPA2-Enterprise). Look f=
or Message 1 from the AP followed by no Message 2 from the client, or Messa=
ge 2 followed by a deauthentication from the AP.</p>
<h2 id=3D"analyzing-retransmissions-and-performance">Analyzing Retransmissi=
ons and Performance<a href=3D"https://sageaxe.com/blog/wireshark-wifi-packe=
t-capture#analyzing-retransmissions-and-performance" class=3D"heading-ancho=
r" aria-label=3D"Copy link to this section"><svg width=3D"15" height=3D"15"=
 viewBox=3D"0 0 24 24" fill=3D"none" stroke=3D"currentColor" stroke-width=
=3D"2" stroke-linecap=3D"round" stroke-linejoin=3D"round" aria-hidden=3D"tr=
ue"><path d=3D"M10 13a5 5 0 007.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 00-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"></=
path><path d=3D"M14 11a5 5 0 00-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 007.07 7.07l1.71-1.71">=
</path></svg></a></h2>
<p>Retransmissions are the single most important metric visible in a wirele=
ss packet capture. Every retransmitted frame consumes airtime twice (or mor=
e), reducing the effective throughput for all clients on the channel [IEEE =
802.11-2020, Section 10.3.4.7].</p>
<p><strong>Calculating Retransmission Rate</strong>:</p>
<p>Use Wireshark=E2=80=99s I/O Graph or Statistics &gt; Conversations to ca=
lculate the ratio:</p>
<pre class=3D"astro-code github-dark-dimmed" style=3D"background-color:#222=
72e;color:#adbac7; overflow-x: auto;" tabindex=3D"0" data-language=3D"plain=
text"><code><span class=3D"line"><span># Display filter for all frames from=
 a specific client</span></span>
<span class=3D"line"><span>wlan.sa =3D=3D aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff</span></span>
<span class=3D"line"><span></span></span>
<span class=3D"line"><span># Display filter for retransmissions only from t=
hat client</span></span>
<span class=3D"line"><span>wlan.sa =3D=3D aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff &amp;&amp; wlan=
.fc.retry =3D=3D 1</span></span></code><button class=3D"copy-code-btn">Copy=
</button></pre>
<p>Compare the frame counts. A retransmission rate above 10% indicates sign=
ificant RF problems =E2=80=94 co-channel interference, low SNR, or multipat=
h. Rates above 20% will cause noticeable application performance degradatio=
n.</p>
<p><strong>MCS Index and Rate Adaptation</strong>: The radiotap header incl=
udes the MCS index (for HT), VHT MCS (for VHT), and HE MCS (for HE/Wi-Fi 6)=
 of each frame. A client that frequently drops to low MCS values is experie=
ncing poor signal conditions. Use this display filter to find frames at low=
 rates:</p>
<pre class=3D"astro-code github-dark-dimmed" style=3D"background-color:#222=
72e;color:#adbac7; overflow-x: auto;" tabindex=3D"0" data-language=3D"plain=
text"><code><span class=3D"line"><span>wlan_radio.11ac.mcs &lt; 3</span></s=
pan></code><button class=3D"copy-code-btn">Copy</button></pre>
<p>Rate adaptation algorithms in the AP and client firmware adjust the MCS =
based on frame delivery success. A spike in retransmissions is typically fo=
llowed by a drop in MCS as the rate adaptation algorithm backs off to more =
robust modulation [IEEE 802.11-2020, Section 10.7].</p>
<h2 id=3D"decrypting-encrypted-traffic">Decrypting Encrypted Traffic<a href=
=3D"https://sageaxe.com/blog/wireshark-wifi-packet-capture#decrypting-encry=
pted-traffic" class=3D"heading-anchor" aria-label=3D"Copy link to this sect=
ion"><svg width=3D"15" height=3D"15" viewBox=3D"0 0 24 24" fill=3D"none" st=
roke=3D"currentColor" stroke-width=3D"2" stroke-linecap=3D"round" stroke-li=
nejoin=3D"round" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=3D"M10 13a5 5 0 007.54.54l3-3=
a5 5 0 00-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"></path><path d=3D"M14 11a5 5 0 00-7.54-.54l=
-3 3a5 5 0 007.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"></path></svg></a></h2>
<p>By default, 802.11 data frames captured in monitor mode are encrypted. W=
ireshark can decrypt them if you provide the key [Wireshark Wiki, =E2=80=9C=
How to Decrypt 802.11=E2=80=9D]:</p>
<p><strong>WPA2-Personal</strong>: Go to Edit &gt; Preferences &gt; Protoco=
ls &gt; IEEE 802.11, click Edit next to Decryption keys, and add a <code>wp=
a-pwd</code> entry with the format <code>passphrase:SSID</code>. Wireshark =
must capture the complete 4-way handshake for a client to decrypt that clie=
nt=E2=80=99s traffic.</p>
<p><strong>WPA2-Enterprise</strong>: Decryption requires the PMK (Pairwise =
Master Key) for each session. This is not practical for troubleshooting enc=
rypted payloads. For enterprise troubleshooting, capture the unencrypted ma=
nagement and EAP frames, and use server-side logs for application-layer ana=
lysis.</p>
<p><strong>Open Networks and OWE</strong>: Frames on open networks (no encr=
yption) are readable without any keys. OWE-encrypted frames require the DH =
key exchange to be captured.</p>
<h2 id=3D"practical-troubleshooting-scenarios">Practical Troubleshooting Sc=
